  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1216 PM MDT Thu Aug 20 2026\n\nSome morning convection occurred as a weak shortwave moved \nthrough the area, exiting by mid morning. High pressure remains \nin place across the area with residual moisture trapped \nunderneath the high allowing for isolated to scattered afternoon\nconvection over the high terrain. A vort max does look to track\nacross the north this evening into the overnight which could \nresult in some lingering activity, but overall should be a hot \nand dry day otherwise. Friday begins a transition day with a low\npressure trough approaching the Pacific Northwest coast. \nHowever, high pressure still remains anchored over the Four \nCorners region, providing another day of scattered showers and \nthunderstorms mainly over the high terrain. The main concerns \nwith any storm activity remain gusty outflow winds to 35 mph and\nlightning with some localized wetting rain in the stronger \nconvection. While hydro concerns will be minimal through Friday \ndue to the drier low levels, stronger convection could still \nresult in an isolated flash flood risk over burn scar areas. The\nmain point of interest as we close out the work week will be \nthe abnormally warm temperatures underneath this high pressure, \nwith triple digits possible in many valley locations Thursday \nand Friday, particularly the lower desert valleys of eastern \nUtah and west-central Colorado. Because of the lingering \nmoisture in the Intermountain West, cloud cover will play a role\nin just how hot these temperatures will be allowed to get, but \nregardless, look for highs to remain about 10 degrees above \nnormal until we hit the weekend.\n\nWET WEATHER RETURNS THIS WEEKEND:\n\nSaturday begins the ramp up in wet weather returning to the \nregion as the low pressure trough pushes inland across the \nPacific Northwest and nudges the high pressure ridge eastward, \nputting us in a more favorable trajectory for monsoonal moisture\nto surge back northward in southerly flow. While the strongest \nportion of this moisture plume will push across the Great Basin \nto our west with PWAT anomalies increasing towards 200 percent \nof normal, PWAT will increase towards an inch or greater across \nour CWA on Saturday, which is still a good jump from where it \nstands currently. Dew points also rise well into the 50s for \nmuch of the area. Hydro impacts become more probable during this\nperiod as a robust shortwave moves through the southerly flow \nlate Saturday afternoon into the evening across eastern Utah and\nmoves through northwest Colorado over the top of the ridge by \nSaturday evening and overnight. This added forcing combined with\nthe increase in moisture could result in more widespread storm \nactivity late in the day on Saturday and potentially continuing \novernight, including a better chance of wetting rain in the \nlower elevations as well. The aforementioned Pacific Northwest \ntrough looks to move across the northern Rockies Sunday, so with\nmoisture already in place, this will result in more scattered \nthunderstorm development as the flow becomes more westerly. Due \nto the projected placement of this shot of moisture, there`s \nstill wiggle room for models to nudge it further into our CWA, \nso don`t count out a more widespread event. As guidance stands \nnow though, the flash flood risk appears more moderate with \nhighest potential over the burn scar areas. Hi-res models will \nreveal much more insight into this system, so stay tuned as we \nbegin to receive this model guidance over the coming days.\n\nIt does appear like drier air tries to work back in for early \nto mid next week as the high pressure shifts back a bit westward\nand builds overhead. However, lingering moisture will still \nresult in daily afternoon thunderstorms favoring the high \nterrain but the coverage does not appear as widespread as this \nweekend with the deeper moisture and more robust forcing in \nplace. Temperatures are expected to cool a bit closer to normal \nthis weekend into early next week with the increase in clouds \nand precipitation.",
